<Project>

    <!-- Pull in the repo-root props (version + shared package metadata) first: GetPathOfFileAbove
         starts the search in the PARENT of this directory, so it finds the root file rather than
         re-importing this one. -->
    <Import Project="$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.props', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))" />

    <!-- Half of the supply-chain lock-file policy: which projects MAINTAIN a lock file. Scoped to src/
         (the four that pack, the rule pack, the two assemblies the contract package carries, and the two the tool
         and the xUnit adapter carry) — plus
         an inline copy in the tests csproj, since tests live outside this directory. It is deliberately
         NOT at the repo root: FixtureRestorer restores throwaway fixture-solution copies under a
         project's bin/ mid-test-run without stripping $(CI), and those copies walk up to whatever props
         file sits above them — a root-level policy would demand lock files for the fixtures and fail
         their CI restore. RestorePackagesWithLockFile makes restore generate/maintain
         packages.lock.json.

         The other half — RestoreLockedMode, which fails a CI restore when the resolved graph drifts
         from the committed lock file — is NOT here. Every tracked csproj declares it for itself, because
         OpenSSF Scorecard's Pinned-Dependencies check reads csproj files only, never an imported props
         file, and credits the property all-or-nothing across every csproj in the tree; declared here it
         was invisible to the check. Where no lock file sits beside a project the
         property is inert, which is why it costs the fixtures nothing. RestoreLockSiteTests holds every
         tracked csproj to carrying it. Local restores stay unlocked either way, so adding a package
         updates the lock naturally. -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- ContinuousIntegrationBuild makes release builds deterministic (normalised paths, stable
         SourceLink). $(CI) is set to 'true' by GitHub Actions, so it only bites in CI. Scoped here
         rather than at the repo root for the same reason as the lock policy above, and the root file
         spells out what went wrong when it was not: it implies DeterministicSourcePaths, which needs
         SourceRoot items that a non-SDK-style fixture project cannot have — and the fixtures are
         staged inside the repo tree, so they inherit whatever sits above them. -->
    <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">
        <ContinuousIntegrationBuild>true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- Whether the analyzers are loaded at all, which decides whether the severities below are read by
         anything. The SDK turns its own analyzers on only from net5.0 up, and five of the nine projects
         here target netstandard2.0, so without this those promotions are inert exactly where they were
         wanted, and silent about it. Declared once here rather than per project: four of the five
         netstandard projects had to remember it by hand and the fifth, the largest, did not, which is
         the whole failure mode. EnforceExtendedAnalyzerRules stays per project — it asks a different
         question, about what the compiler will host. -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <EnableNETAnalyzers>true</EnableNETAnalyzers>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- Analyzer severities, from the repo-root analyzers.globalconfig (which explains what it promotes,
         why, and why it is not named .globalconfig). Naming it here is what scopes it to src/, the way
         the policies above are scoped: nothing auto-discovers it, so the fixture copies staged under
         bin/ never inherit it. The tests csproj names the same file for itself. -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <GlobalAnalyzerConfigFiles Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../analyzers.globalconfig" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!-- The build analyzer, run over this repository's own projects against the committed
         sliderule.model.json at the root, which the package's targets find by searching upward. It is
         the last released package and never a reference to the project beside it: Core and Extraction
         cannot be analyzed by an analyzer built from themselves in the same build, an editor does not
         reload an analyzer that was rebuilt under it, and this way the model file is read by the
         previous release's reader, so a format change that release cannot read reds here first. The
         version names the last published release, as PackageValidationBaselineVersion below does, and
         moves with it.

         Six projects take it as an ordinary private PackageReference, the way a consumer does. The
         analyzer project and the two it references, Core and Extraction, cannot: NuGet walks a project's
         references for cycles before PrivateAssets prunes anything, so a reference in either of those
         two reaches the analyzer project's own graph as the package of its own name, and restore fails
         with NU1108. Those three download the package instead, which keeps it out of the dependency
         graph, and src/Directory.Build.targets hands the compiler what a reference would have: the
         three assemblies as Analyzer items and the package's own targets file. A download is recorded
         in no lock file, which is why the version is pinned exactly there. -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <SlideRuleAnalyzersVersion>0.1.0</SlideRuleAnalyzersVersion>
        <_SlideRuleAnalyzersByDownload Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'SlideRule' Or '$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'SlideRule.Extraction' Or '$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'SlideRule.Analyzers'">true</_SlideRuleAnalyzersByDownload>
    </PropertyGroup>
    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(_SlideRuleAnalyzersByDownload)' != 'true'">
        <PackageReference Include="SlideRule.Analyzers" Version="$(SlideRuleAnalyzersVersion)" PrivateAssets="all" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(_SlideRuleAnalyzersByDownload)' == 'true'">
        <PackageDownload Include="SlideRule.Analyzers" Version="[$(SlideRuleAnalyzersVersion)]" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!-- The compiler writes each project's XML documentation file, and the packages carry it beside
         the assembly, so a consumer's editor shows the doc comments the source already carries. The
         file is also what turns the documentation-mode diagnostics on: without it the compiler
         resolves no cref and reports no missing comment, so a namespace move can leave crefs pointing
         at nothing through a green build and a green suite. That whole family is promoted to an
         error here, not CS1591 alone, so the build holds both halves of the invariant: every public
         member is documented, and every comment is well-formed and resolves. A comment naming a type
         that no longer exists is as broken as a missing one. Scoped to src/ for the same reason as
         the policies above: the tests project would report thousands of missing comments on its
         public test members, and the fixture copies staged under bin/ must inherit nothing from
         here. -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
        <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);CS0419;CS1570;CS1571;CS1572;CS1573;CS1574;CS1580;CS1581;CS1584;CS1587;CS1589;CS1590;CS1591;CS1592;CS1710;CS1711;CS1712;CS1723;CS1734;CS1735</WarningsAsErrors>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- NU1701 is restore resolving a package through the .NET Framework fallback: the project then
         compiles against assemblies built for another framework, and their Framework polyfills join
         the graph. The MSBuild packages are the known case, since each MSBuild minor line ships assets
         for its own .NET only and the next line carries none for this target framework, so a version
         bump that leaves the line fails restore here instead of building green on the wrong assets.
         Restore reads this property too, so the promotion holds before anything compiles. Scoped to
         src/ for the same reason as the policies above. -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1701</WarningsAsErrors>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- Package validation's baseline and mode, shared by the two packages that switch validation on in
         their own csproj: the contract and the xUnit adapter, the two assemblies a consumer's own project
         compiles against. The Core csproj says why validation runs at all. What the two share is written
         here, so the one number a release moves has one site.

         Strict mode compares in both directions, so an ADDITION reports as well as a removal. That is the
         half worth paying for: a removal breaks a consumer loudly at their next build, while an addition is
         the thing that silently becomes permanent. It is also what lets the committed surface pins in tests/
         stay at type-and-enum grain rather than enumerating every member: with member additions reported
         here, pinning them there would move those files on roughly half of all commits to say the same
         thing twice.

         What this check does NOT give is locality. It runs at pack time, which in practice is one CI job and
         the release gate, so the pins exist to red in the suite beside the edit. An enum renumbering reports
         here as CP0011 and without strict mode, being a break rather than a growth (measured 2026-09-12),
         but it is also the one surface change that breaks no compile anywhere, so it is the one most worth
         catching in seconds.

         The baseline lags <Version> by design: it names the last PUBLISHED version, which is one number for
         both packages because they release in lockstep. Between releases it sits equal to <Version> and the
         accumulated delta is visible. Bumping it is part of the release ritual, deliberately NOT a fifth site
         in ManifestVersionTests' four-way version equality; PublicSurfaceSyncTests asserts only that it is no
         newer, and that no csproj overrides it.

         Acknowledgement is a CompatibilitySuppressions.xml beside the csproj it acknowledges for, regenerated
         with -p:ApiCompatGenerateSuppressionFile=true and reviewed per symbol. A package with nothing to
         acknowledge carries none.

         The group is unconditioned on purpose and inert in every project that does not validate: the SDK
         gates both the baseline's download and the comparison on EnablePackageValidation and IsPackable. A
         condition on $(EnablePackageValidation) or $(PackageId) would never hold here, because this file is
         evaluated before a project body sets either. -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <PackageValidationBaselineVersion>0.1.0</PackageValidationBaselineVersion>
        <EnableStrictModeForBaselineValidation>true</EnableStrictModeForBaselineValidation>
    </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
